**Accepted deferred implementation.** Runtime anonymous records are queued after
shared MON syntax and numeric semantics. The examples below describe the accepted
contract, not current executable support. See @../progress (Progress).

A runtime anonymous record is a local value backed by a hidden nominal struct.
It uses the named-only form of @../language-overview/#mon-syntax (MON syntax).
The ordinary struct owners provide construction, field access, copy, borrow
validation and lifetime validation.

### Construction

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Size = |
        width Int,
        height Int,
    |

    show_window ||:
        window ~= (
            title = "Strategy",
            bounds = (
                size = Size(width = 1280, height = 720),
                visible = true,
            ),
        )

        window.bounds.size.width = 1440
        independent ~= copy window
        independent.title = "Overview"
    ;
]

A runtime receiving context selects a runtime record. Its field expressions
need not fold. Every field has a unique name and an initializer. At least one
field is required. Empty `()` is outside this runtime surface.

Field initializers run once in source order under ordinary expression, access
and error rules. Labels introduce no local names. A mutable root permits field
writes only when ordinary access rules allow them. It does not copy an existing
child or grant additional authority over one.

### Hidden nominal identity

Each static literal site owns one hidden nominal type within its compiled body.
Repeated executions of that site produce values of the same type. Distinct
sites remain different types even when their field names and types match.
Aliases and copies preserve the type identity.

Compatible assignment requires the same resolved identity. Another literal of
matching shape is not a replacement value of that type. The hidden type has no
source-visible name, constructor name, receiver methods or trait conformance.

A named struct still uses its explicit constructor. An anonymous record does
not satisfy a named field or parameter by matching its shape.

### Recursive local composition

Anonymous parents may contain anonymous children directly or through an
already bound local. They may also contain ordinary supported values, including
explicitly constructed nominal structs and choices.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    show_state ||:
        child = (count = 3)
        state = (
            saved = child,
            fresh = (enabled = true),
        )

        selected = state.fresh
        count = state.saved.count
    ;
]

A compiler-generated parent field carries the child's resolved hidden type.
Nesting is supported recursively from the first runtime implementation.
Extracting a child to another local preserves its type and alias relationship.
An ordinary named or scalar leaf follows that leaf type's ordinary rules.

This composition permission applies to compiler-generated anonymous parent
fields. It does not make hidden types legal in authored nominal field declarations
or permit an unnamed child to bypass a named parent's declared field types.

### Local-only uses

Supported uses are local binding, compatible assignment, projection, explicit
copy and recursive composition inside anonymous parents. Normal type and
access rules still apply.

A hidden identity cannot cross a function argument or return boundary, including
private functions. It cannot appear in an authored signature, type alias,
nominal struct or choice field, receiver method, trait evidence or exported
value/interface. The initial implementation also rejects collection/map storage
and generic arguments carrying a hidden identity.

These restrictions are transitive. A containing parent, optional or captured
value cannot hide a prohibited identity. An ordinary leaf extracted from a
record is not restricted merely because it came through that record.

A generic body may construct a local record from its resolved concrete values
where ordinary compilation supports that body. Passing the hidden type as a
generic argument remains invalid.

### Values and capabilities

Records follow ordinary reference, copy, borrow and lifetime rules. Inline
nesting creates no new region, implicit deep copy or escape exemption.

Compile-time records remain separate folded member groups, with the whole-value
runtime restrictions in @../constants/#const-records (Const records). A runtime
record never uses the compile-time record marker as its runtime type.

Wiring retains its own storage rules. MON construction does not turn wire
identity or routes into ordinary storable field values. Runtime records gain no
serialisation support from sharing MON syntax.
